# LESSON: HTTPS, and who you can trust
# =====================================
# HTTPS does two jobs:
#   1. ENCRYPTION: nobody else on the Wi-Fi can read what the Pi (or your
#      phone) says to Bolt, including the Pi's key and everything you say.
#   2. IDENTITY: the Pi can be sure it's really talking to Bolt, not an imposter.
#
# For identity, Bolt shows a CERTIFICATE, signed by someone the Pi trusts.
# Websites get theirs signed by big public companies. Bolt lives at a home
# address (192.168.4.58) that no public company will vouch for, so Caddy runs
# its own little CERTIFICATE AUTHORITY: a private "stamp of approval". Each
# device that should trust Bolt gets a copy of that stamp (bolt-ca.crt), once.
#
# Caddy sits in FRONT of Bolt (a "reverse proxy"): it handles the encryption
# and passes each request on to Bolt inside Docker's private network.
{
	local_certs # sign certificates with Caddy's own certificate authority
	skip_install_trust # don't try to install it on the mini PC itself
	default_sni {$BOLT_ADDRESS} # devices connecting by IP address don't say which name they want
}

https://{$BOLT_ADDRESS}, https://localhost {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy bolt:8770 {
		flush_interval -1 # pass Bolt's streamed sentences on immediately, don't save them up
	}
}
